## Where this repo is, and what to do first

`asgard-cli` scaffolded this repo. What exists is the platform contract (this
file), the acceptance gate under `scripts/`, the docs structure, and an empty
chart skeleton per project. **Everything customer-specific is still to be
written**, including the sections of this file marked TODO.

Read `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/` first. If its module index is empty, no behaviour
has been settled yet and the first job is the three decisions below.

**Before any conversation with the customer, run the briefing for it:**

    asgard-cli brief customer-meeting

**The riskiest thing in this engagement leaves no trace in this repository.**
Talking to a customer changes no file, so nothing here and nothing derived from
here prepares anybody for it. Every entry in `asgard-cli brief` is there because
somebody actually got it wrong - `write-chart` before touching a chart,
`handover` before telling anyone it is live.

**Read what this repository records before deciding anything.** Each of these
reads one file back, and each takes `--format json`:

    asgard-cli question      what nobody has answered yet, and who each is with
    asgard-cli request       what the customer asked for and is not done
    asgard-cli task          the task specs that are open
    asgard-cli project       what each chart declares, and what its shape lacks

**No command says where this engagement is, and none will.** There is no step
you are on - one that already has every requirement is at no step at all. What
the work is made of, below, is a map and not an order. `asgard-cli guide <name>`
reads one piece of guidance at any time, and `asgard-cli find` reaches them by
subject.

| name | the work | produces |
|---|---|---|
| `requirements` | inventory the customer's systems, and who talks to each one | `docs/meeting-notes/`, then a `docs/decisions/` record |
| `projects` | decide how the work splits into projects | `projects/<slug>/` via `asgard-cli project add` |
| `data-sources` | wire up the databases | `.env`, `DB_TARGETS` in `scripts/db/pgenv.py`, `DataConnector` CRs |
| `read-path` | **decide each project's read path** | `SemanticLayer`, or a fixed-query `Toolset` |
| `entry-point` | **decide each project's entry point** | the agent hub, or `BotProvider` -> `Workflow` - or nothing, if the shape has none |
| `knowledge` | **decide where unstructured knowledge lives**, if there is any | a `SourceSet` Drive |
| `verify` | run the gate | see Acceptance Gate |
| `deploy` | deploy | see Deploy / CI |

Not every chart ends with an entry point. Which CR kinds finish one depends on
what this project is for, which the files cannot say and no tool can check -
so say it in the chart next to what makes it unusual. `asgard-cli size` lists
the shapes an engagement chooses between.

Steps 4, 5 and 6 are the three decisions in the next section. Each one has an
answer that looks obvious and is wrong.

### The CLI

`asgard-cli` scaffolded this repo and stays useful afterwards:

| command | when |
|---|---|
| `asgard-cli gate` | **after changing anything under a chart or `.asgard-pipeline.yaml`, and before pushing.** Everything this machine can check, in one command. A repository of custom resources has no compiler; this is the nearest thing to one, and it is one command so that it cannot go stale the way a checklist does |
| `asgard-cli brief <what>` | **before a meeting, a chart change or a handover.** The activity you are about to do, and how it has been got wrong |
| `asgard-cli question` | what nobody has answered yet, and who each is waiting on. **Read this before designing anything** |
| `asgard-cli request`, `asgard-cli task` | what the customer asked for, and the task specs open against it |
| `asgard-cli project` | what each chart declares, and what its declared shape still lacks. It reports no step, because there is none |
| `asgard-cli guide <name>` | the guidance for one decision; `asgard-cli guide` lists it |
| `asgard-cli find <terms>` | **start here for a question.** Searches the wiki, the shapes, the guidance and the skills together; ask in Chinese if that is the language it was asked in |
| `asgard-cli usecase` | **before authoring any CR.** How each shape is built, taken from deployments already in production: when to use it, the skeleton, the fields that are not obvious, and what the mistake cost someone. `--search <terms>` finds a shape from what the customer asked for |
| `asgard-cli check` | one step of `gate`, when that is the step you are fixing |
| `asgard-cli project add <slug>` | the engagement discovers a new project |
| `asgard-cli scaffold` | after adding a project, to write its chart skeleton. Safe to re-run - it never overwrites a file that already exists, so edits to this file survive |
| `asgard-cli skill update` | **when the platform says the reference material here is behind.** It writes what the Asgard server this repository deploys to actually enforces - the processor catalogue and its config keys - into `.agents/skills/`. Commit what it writes; the diff is the changelog |

The stage is derived from what is on disk - which files exist, which CR kinds
each chart declares - not from a counter. It stays right when someone does a step
by hand, and it cannot claim work is finished that is not.

`.asgard-config.json` holds the workspace slug and the project list. The
repository name and every namespace are **derived** from that slug rather than
stored, so edit it through the CLI rather than by hand.

## The three decisions that get answered wrong

Each has an obvious answer, and a correct answer that cost a rewrite. The
reasoning does not survive in the CRs themselves, which is why it is here.

**Every one of them follows from the same question: who is on the other end?**
An internal caller that can authenticate and an anonymous visitor need different
shapes, and the difference is not a preference.

### 1. The read path: semantic layer, or fixed query tools

| audience | answer |
|---|---|
| internal, authenticated | `SemanticLayer`, one per source system, one Agent each |
| public, anonymous | a `Toolset` of **zero-parameter** fixed queries, no semantic layer |

**Why a semantic layer is wrong for a public audience.** Mounted without
`allowedCubes` it lets the agent compose arbitrary SQL over every cube in it,
and **the exposed surface grows by itself each time a cube is added** - nobody
goes back to narrow it. Excluding the sensitive tables is not the fix: the shape
itself is the risk.

With fixed tools, what can be asked is decided by a few statements in version
control, no user input reaches SQL so the injection surface is zero, and
widening it takes a CR change and a review.

> Answered wrong once: a public site got a `SemanticLayer` first, and it was
> removed and replaced with five zero-parameter query tools.

**"Zero-parameter" means the model supplies nothing** - not that every caller
sees the same rows. An anonymous channel can still answer "where is MY repair
case": the identity is injected server-side on every turn, arriving in the
request the front end sends rather than as a tool argument the model fills in,
and the query filters on it. The model still picks only *which question*, so the
injection surface is still zero. Whatever sits in front is what knows who is
talking; if nothing in front knows, the per-customer half cannot be built at all,
and that is a question for the customer. See `asgard-cli usecase
per-turn-credentials`, which also carries the failure mode - a query that forgets
to filter on the injected identity fails on the anonymous path only, and that is
the path nobody tests.

**One more trap on the tools themselves.** Two tools sitting on the same
FROM/JOIN and differing only in projection force each one's `description` to
name the other as the alternative - which is exactly where a model picks wrong.
Merge them and make the difference a column value instead of a tool choice.

### 2. The entry point: agent hub, or your own BotProvider

| audience | answer |
|---|---|
| a caller that can authenticate to the platform | the platform's per-namespace `preset-agent-hub`, with an `Agent` CR per system. You author no BotProvider |
| an anonymous visitor | your own `BotProvider` -> `Workflow` -> `SandboxBlueprint` |

**Why the agent hub cannot serve a public widget.** An `Agent` CR is reachable
only through `preset-agent-hub`, and that endpoint requires an authenticated
caller. `BotProvider.entrypoint` accepts a `Workflow`, **never an `Agent`**. So
a public audience forces the second shape, no matter how it is configured.

In that shape the capabilities live **one CR further out**, on the
`SandboxBlueprint`: its own `toolsetNames` / `skillSetNames` / `sourceSetMounts`.
Searching the `Workflow` CRD for the link finds nothing, and that has produced a
wrong conclusion before.

A single public agent needs **no subagent**. Attaching one through the
blueprint's `agents[]` only adds a hop where the orchestrator restates the
question and restates the answer back.

> Answered wrong once: a public widget was specced onto the agent hub, then
> re-decided as a self-hosted chain.

### 3. Unstructured knowledge: a SourceSet Drive

There is one answer: a `SourceSet` with `spec.contextIndex`, mounted read-only
on the `SandboxBlueprint`, queried with the sandbox's builtin **graphify** skill.

This is a recommendation, not a platform rule. `KnowledgeBase`, `Loader`,
`Indexer` and `Source` are all live CRDs with no deprecation marker, and the
console ships the feature. What stands behind the recommendation is one
engagement that built the Loader-and-retrieval-workflow way and rebuilt it on a
Drive. An older chart containing one is a shape somebody chose before that.

Setting `contextIndex` is the whole switch: the reconciler derives three
same-named CRs that mount the Drive writable and run the indexer on a cron.
Clearing the field tears them down and renames the index aside - **to pause it,
label the SourceSet `context-index-suspend`, do not remove the field**.

> Answered wrong once: knowledge was built on `KnowledgeBase` + `Loader` +
> retrieval workflows, and the whole mechanism was removed.

### When you settle one of these

Write a `docs/decisions/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md` for it **on the day it is
settled**, and say why the rejected option was rejected. That sentence is the
only part nobody can reconstruct later, and it is what stops the next engagement
from making the same choice.

